From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FramePlayer contributors <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 15:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/13] xr: Linux OpenXR doesn't wait for the GPU each frame

The Vulkan binding waited on the CPU twice per frame: in WaitOnSyncFd
until the page's GL work was done, and in CopyToSwapchainImage until the
copy into the swapchain was done. The render loop only hands the page
its next frame after the current one is submitted, so the page's CPU
work and the GPU's work for the previous frame never overlapped: a frame
took page time + GPU time. Fire Fight 2 (3 ms of script, 16 ms of GPU)
ran at a third of 108 Hz instead of half.

- WaitOnSyncFd only imports the page-done sync file as a semaphore; the
  copy's submit waits on it, on the GPU.
- CopyToSwapchainImage submits without waiting. OpenXR only needs the
  work submitted to the session's queue before xrReleaseSwapchainImage.
  Each intermediate image has its own command buffer and fence, and keeps
  the semaphore its copy waited on until that fence signals.
- WaitForImage() waits for an image's last copy before the page draws
  into it again (OnSwapchainImageReady) or it is destroyed; by then it is
  normally long done.
- OpenXR requires the GPU to be done with a swapchain before
  xrDestroySwapchain, and a copy still writing into a destroyed swapchain
  image faulted the GPU (Steam Frame kernel: "gpu fault ... dir=WRITE",
  then every GL context on the device was lost and Chromium blocked
  WebGL). OpenXrGraphicsBinding::OnSwapchainDestroying(), called before
  xrDestroySwapchain (also when the session ends), lets the Vulkan binding
  wait for its copies.

Without a GPU fence (CHROMIUM_gpu_fence; ANGLE's OpenGL backend under X11
has none) the render loop still glFinish()es before the copy, so pages
only overlap with the GPU on ANGLE's Vulkan backend, which the Frame
launcher selects. Fire Fight 2 with 2x antialiasing at 1728x1728 per eye:
a steady 54 fps (half of 108 Hz, p99 frame 18.9 ms), where ANGLE on
OpenGL over zink ran at 38.

Applies on top of 0009.
